<!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
<section style="margin: 2.5rem 0;" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Date Arithmetic Mistakes</h2>
  <ul style="color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.8; margin-left: 1.25rem;">
    <li><strong>Equating 30 Days to 1 Month:</strong> Adding "1 month" is not identical to adding "30 days". Adding 30 days to January 1 lands on January 31, while adding 1 month lands on February 1.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting Weekend Extension in Business Days:</strong> Adding 5 business days always spans at least 7 calendar days because it spans a weekend. Adding 10 business days spans at least 14 calendar days.</li>
    <li><strong>Month-End Overflows:</strong> Without proper month-end clamping, adding 1 month to March 31 could slip into May 1 on primitive date libraries. Our tool clamps directly to April 30.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<div class="article-content">
  <h2 id="article-heading">Why Is Calculating Target Dates Essential?</h2>
  <p>Accurate date arithmetic is a fundamental requirement across project planning, legal agreements, financial forecasting, and personal organization. Manually counting days on a physical calendar often leads to miscalculations due to varying month lengths, weekend overlaps, and leap year days. Utilizing an automated date addition calculator guarantees exact calendar placement every time.</p>

  <h2>How Does Business Day Calculation Work?</h2>
  <p>When planning business operations, standard calendar days often fail to reflect actual working timelines. Project deliverables, payment terms, and legal notice periods routinely specify business days. When the <strong>Business days only</strong> option is active, our calculator skips Saturdays and Sundays. For example, adding 5 business days to a Friday advances through Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, and reaches target completion on the following Friday.</p>
</div>

